processor/ARCHITECTURE.md:9:- **Scaling and shutdown:** pods share one queue through the visibility timeout, so no two pods work on the same message. A stopping pod finishes its batch and stops polling; anything it did not delete returns for another pod.
upload-service/ARCHITECTURE.md:7:- **Why each choice was made**, linking to the ADRs in `docs/architecture/decisions/`: defensive header parsing, specific status codes and never 503, the timeout order (S3 write 60 s < ALB 120 s < Tomcat 130 s), and the per-pod rate limit.
local/CLAUDE.md:50:- Redrive after 5 receives and a 60 s visibility timeout are queue settings in `init-aws.sh`, never Java constants (ADR 0023).
local/CLAUDE.md:59:- Keep `proxy_100_continue: true` and the `/logs/` route's `timeout: 0s`. Without the first, Envoy sends 100 Continue itself and the phone sends the body before any check runs (ADR 0009). Envoy's default route timeout of 15 s, counted from the end of the body, would cut off an S3 write that takes up to 60 s (ADR 0005).
local/CLAUDE.md:63:- The Poly root in `poly-ca-bundle.pem` comes from pki.polycom.com, SHA-256 `5A:9A:1F:57:09:02:08:B9:AC:DA:4F:23:95:10:15:39:77:AE:A2:10:89:79:15:01:4D:43:1E:FF:06:60:1E:BE`. After changing the bundle, check that a phone's chain still passes `openssl verify -CAfile local/envoy/poly-ca-bundle.pem`.
